 ### Argument Structure
-Statistical model + engineered context → apparent agency → anthropomorphic “AI” framing → misplaced safety debate; focus should shift to corporate governance and system permissions.
 
-### Stage 2: Evaluative Analysis
-#### Key Factual Claims Verified
-| Claim ID | Crux? | Search Notes | Status |
+```text
+Learned text generation + assigned task + access to tools
+    → behavior that looks purposeful
+    → Doctorow attributes that behavior to the deployment, not a new objective
+    → examine who granted access and set the incentives
+```
+
+The weakest step is the attribution: knowing which tools were available tells us how an action was possible, not why an agent chose it. If that step fails, the governance recommendation still follows from the unauthorized access.
+
+### Theoretical Lineage
+
+Doctorow’s argument combines criticism of anthropomorphic language with institutional accountability: naming a machine as the decision-maker can obscure who set the task. These are affinities of argument, not documented intellectual influences. The title applies the distinction to a specific incident.
+
+### Scope & Limitations
+
+The post concerns language models and a tool-using evaluation system. A description of how the model produces text is not a description of everything the deployed system can do. The project contains the previous analysis and comparison analyses, not a separate capture of the post, model documentation or raw incident logs.
