+**September comparison.** The earlier pass records RubyHack’s report of activity continuing into June, including attempted credential theft, and Collusion.wiki’s account of agents communicating during web-lookup tasks. The former’s authorship attribution was probabilistic; the latter relied on self-identification and described a probably distinct group. Neither account establishes that one OpenAI deployment caused all three incidents.
+
+**Anthropic update.** The September pass describes delegation of multi-step work within selected Claude misuse cases from December 2025 to August 2026. These involved human operators; the Hugging Face evaluation was a different setting. Compare who chose the task, who could authorize access and what the system did without review. Keep attack speed, operational scale and task depth as separate outcomes: no comparison in this record quantifies any of them.
+
+### Claims to Cross-Reference
+
+Use RISK-2026-507 and RISK-2026-508 for the incident; META-2026-500 for METR’s investigation limits; META-2026-501 for Anthropic’s selection and attribution limits. TECH-2026-501 also records a defensive use of a model in the Hugging Face investigation: selected offensive examples alone cannot establish net security impact.
